counterfactuals.output:
We investigate phonon-mediated transitions between the center-of-mass translational states of a cesium atom interacting with a planar silica surface. The atomic motion normal to the interface is quantized in the adsorption potential, producing discrete bound states and a continuum of free states, while surface vibrations are represented by a three-dimensional Debye phonon bath. In the Born–Markov approximation, the state populations obey the Pauli master equation
\[
\dot P_i=\sum_j\!\left(\Gamma_{j\to i}P_j-\Gamma_{i\to j}P_i\right).
\]
Expanding the atom–surface potential to first order in the normal surface displacement gives transition rates
\[
\Gamma_{i\to j}=
\frac{|\langle j|V'(z)|i\rangle|^2\,\omega_{ij}}
{2\pi\rho\hbar}
\sum_\lambda v_\lambda^{-3}
\begin{cases}
n(\omega_{ij})+1,&E_i>E_j,\\
n(\omega_{ij}),&E_i<E_j,
\end{cases}
\]
for \(\omega_{ij}<\omega_D\), and zero otherwise. Here \(\rho\), \(v_\lambda\), and \(\omega_D\) are the silica mass density, sound velocities, and Debye cutoff, respectively, and \(n(\omega)\) is the Bose–Einstein occupation factor. Numerical solution of the cesium–silica eigenproblem and evaluation of the force matrix elements show strongly state-dependent rates spanning several orders of magnitude. Transitions among weakly bound levels dominate thermal redistribution, whereas deeply bound states are protected when their level spacings exceed the Debye energy. Continuum-to-bound processes yield finite phonon-assisted trapping rates for incident atoms, while bound-to-continuum transitions describe thermally activated desorption and become appreciable near room temperature. The calculated rates satisfy detailed balance and provide a quantitative framework for adsorption, desorption, and motional decoherence of cold atoms near dielectric surfaces.
